Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

There has been a lot of discussion regarding 
the quality of the gnu compilers in 
comparison to DU compilers. We have a little
code to evaluate floating point performance for
different loop lengths. The loop looks like   

 do l =1, length
    f(l) = c(1)*(a(l) + c(2)*(a(l) + c(3)*(a(l) + c(4)*
&     (a(l) + c(5)*(a(l) + c(6))))))
 enddo

c is just a array of constants and f and a are variables

We measure the time by calls to system functions 
and look at MFLOPS over loop length. We used different 
compilers, system is LX board/2MB cache and 533MHZ processor.

We were fairly dissapointed with the g77. We used egcs-1.0.3
with haifa enabled as well as egcs-1.0.2. Just to give you 
some numbers

                       MFLOPS

 length     g77/egcs-1.0.3     DU        PA-RISC/8000

  100         107.8            373.3      
~300                           
  10825        78.7            172.3       ~300
  104912       62.4            98.8        

We tried several compile options without success. 
Even a PII 300 was faster for almost all loop
lengths. The PA-RISC has about the same Specfp as
the Alpha.
We know that this is a rather crude test and that
a good compiler can optimize that very well. But 
we are also not satisfied with the performance of the
Alpha for our "real life" codes. Coding that loop in
C yields exactly the same results. This is rather
discouraging especially knowing that Digital released
their compilers for Alpha/NT which gives much better results,
according to www.specbench.org.

I hope that Digital will release their compilers it for Linux.

Christian



