ghsong@NOSPAMkjist.ac.kr (G. Hugh SONG) wrote:

> We bought an LX board with 21164 533MHz CPU and 2MB cache running 
> Linux.  Memory size is 128 MB.

> 1.  Is 21164 cpu for the LX board different from 21164a in the 
>     DEC server [7305] ?

As far as I know not.  However, the memory system is completely different 
from that of the LX boards, and that's an important factor too.

> 2. Is there any possible mistake I could have made in compling 
>    my application, which is not favored by alpha?

Well, it's hard to say without you giving actual command line arguments to 
g77.

> It is a terrible feeling.  I expected that my app runs substantially
> faster on the LX board than on Sun Ultra I.  Now I found that it
> is not so.

I must say I find it surprising too; on the other hand, the Sun Ultra is 
probably much more expensive than the PC164LX.

Another note:  You wrote your code is double complex based.  This is an area 
where the GNU compiling system is known to be weak.  Officially, it has 
support internally for Single Precision Complex and Double Precision Complex 
data, but as this doesn't work flawlessly, the Fortran Frontend doesn't use 
it, but explicitly spells out the complex computations itself to the code 
generation phase.  This tends to produce less well optimised code:  On an 
example posted to the egcs mailing list several months ago, I obtained a 2 
fold speedup by compiling it with -fno-emulate-complex - however, that's 
because I know the problematic support for the complex data types has no 
effect on my NEXT station (m68k-next-nextstep3).

Hope this helps,

