John Rowe <rowe@excc.exeter.ac.uk> wrote:

> cekim@ix.netcom.com writes:

> >Again, you will suffer in accuracy if that is important and if you need 64 
bit
> >numbers, the PII is next to useless as it will have to clunk all day to
> >effectively reconstruct a 64 bit result out of bit sliced 32 bit 
instructions.

> Que? The PII supports 80 bit IEEE maths with 80 bit registers. (Of
> course the fp register system is a mess but that's another story.) All
> of our work is 64 bit floating point and we're very happy with the
> PIIs for the price. I have no idea about 64 bit integers.

Under g77/gcc (whether g77-0.5.23 + gcc-2.8.1 or egcs-1.0.3) you'll get 
support for 64 bit integers:  In C: long long int; in Fortran: INTEGER*8.
Of course, on a thoroughly 32 bit architecture like the ix86 this means that 
simple arithmetic has to be broken down into 32-bit chunks, but it can be 
done transparently.

I don't know off-hand of a modern architecture *not* supporting 64-bit 
floating point arithmetic.

OTOH, you really have to love Intel for having 80-bit intermediate results in 
registers, or alternatively, all those who aren't ashamed of developing 
algorithms that break down on the differences between 64- and 80-bit 
intermediate values.

