Have you considered moving the existing Oses from the current setup to the new
drives efore you trash the old system?

The Alpha does not really the the concept of a boot sector like that of
windows. The ALpha BIOS needs the first partition to be FAT because it reads
this partition to find out what to boot (based onthe command line argument in
the CMOS/flash setup.  I have done this in the x86 world but I have never
tried it with my alpha(should be the same with linux:

Linux:
1. Format new hard drive with ext2 filesystem form running linux system (just
connect it up and use fdisk at command line). then mount the new filesystem
somewhere on the existing system -> mount /dev/sda3 /newdrive

2. as root, copy entire filsystem (cp -rd / /newdrive)  the "d" is to preserve
sym-links.

3. change /newdrive/etc/fstab (on new filesystem) to be as you plan to have
the system when you are done moving things around.  Be sure where you want
things and make sure you have fstab correct or it may leave this filesystem
unbootable (you can recover so long as you have another bootable linux system
available)

4. at this point I would recommend trying to boot the new filesystem before
emaking anymore changes.  just edit the bios pointer to your existing linux
system to point to this new SCSI drive (obviously the /newdrive is meaningless
now, so don't include that)

5. If it works, trash the old linux system and the NT system is next

NT: I assume you have a FAT partition on at the bigining of your first disk
that holds NT.	If this is the case thenyou can reformat the former linux
space as NTFS and you have a new drive.  leave your NT where it is and grow 
on your new D:\ drive.

You could move linux and then reinstall/reformat the other partition with the
NT install (since it is so simple).

If you have a third disk you could relocate NT using linux to copy the files
to prevent NT from complaining about sharing and permission violations (just
make sure to mount the NT partition as "VFAT" before doing this to preserve
the long filenames.  Boot the new NT to be safe and check it out.  Using hte
cloned NT reformat the old NT space and then using linux, move the cloned NT
back.

People claim you cannot move NT, but I have done it several times using linux
(and mounting the NT partition as a VFAT!!)  works jsut fine!

IF you decide to just reinstall everything...

The alpha install is not really too much more complicated, but it has a few
bugs.

You have a few options, and you can install the two OSes in either order.

The linux install involves making:
1. a Milo disk (get from RH /images/milo directory).
2. a boot disk also from /images
3. a ramdisk from (again in /images

of course choose the right one for your system.

The only tricky part here is that you have to go into the AlphaBIOS and tell
it to boot from the floppy (the milo image you created and should now be in
your floppy drive.

I believe the SX is much like the LX here in that you go into the
utilities->OS setup section of the bios, choose the floppy as the drive and
then type \linload.exe for the path.  The second path box (just below where
you typed "linload.exe" simply needs a "\" this feild is ignored by linload.

then once Milo has booted:

take the milo disk out and place the "boot.img" floppy in the drive

type:
boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1

hit enter and it will crunch for a while then ask for a ramdisk (your last
floppy).

The rest is typical redhat with one exception:

insmod is not included on thier install floppy, so you can only configure
what is loaded into the boot kernel by default.  You can compensate for this
with your running system by compiling a kernel with all of the bells and
whistles you need now(be sure to test ot out of course).  Then mount the
boot.img floppy you created and replace the vmlinux.gz with your new and
improved version.  This will eliminatethe need for insmod during hte install
prodecure.

NT: This one is a real	no brainer: 1. place NT dcdrom in drive. 2. go into
bios setup and there should be an option to "install NT" 3. make sure you
have the OEM disk you need to tell the NT install what MB, and HAL you need
along with the onboard IDE controller.	(this should be available at your
vendors sight as a download.  if not see www.dcginc.com I think he has what
you would need somewhere.

From here is is a normal NT install.

hope that helps,

/Mike
