Hi,

Just bought Linix 5.1 Alpha package on CD. May sound like a stupid
question, but am having trouble installing from the CD. The machine is
an Alphaserver 400/4-266 with SRM (DU) console selected. Will be
dedicating a whole disk to Linux, plain vanilla, no clever stuff,
multiboot/os partitioning etc. I also would like to keep the machine
standard in terms of what is in the flash monitor - I may want have DU
or NT on a second disk.

If I boot the CD, I get the `aboot' prompt, but when the kernel filename
/ path is entered, aboot always returns 'file not found'. Have had a
look at the CD using Digital Unix to check the pathnames, so am pretty
sure where the required image is, but no luck from aboot.

The Linux Alpha addendum is not v helpfull - it assumes that everyone
wants to use milo with an NT console and while it is suggested that CD
install is possible, the SRM chapter then says nothing about how this is
achieved. 

Am a newcomer to Linux - have installed unix on other machines (Sun,
Alpha Unix) where a typical sequence of events would be:

1) Plug in and boot CD / tape media

2) load miniroot / kernel / setup program into ramdisk, whatever.

3) Use disklabel to partition / label disk

4) Select options and install OS

5) Reboot and configure system / rebuild kernel as required etc

6) Job done, enjoy Linux.

Nowhere in the manual does it talk about disk partitioning, an essential
step in the install process and, assuming that a cd boot is possible,
says nothing about what cd partition to boot, device naming and a host
of other assumptions and innacuracies which make the doc useless as a
"howto install". If suggested web pages are consulted, only a repeat of
the addendum is found.

Do I expect too much ? - Linux comes shrinkwrapped with docs suggesting
that a cd only (supplied media) install should be possible but it's very
unclear how this is done - any ideas ?.

Regards, Chris



