Those are two very seperate problems!!

The kernel hang is the typical result of an alpha kernel compile before you
work all the bugs out (of the kernel configuration that is).

I am afriad, I cannot offer much help with the MIATA, but in general start
simple with the kernel (ie: no loadable modules, minimal everything).

Then build up.

I ran into two problems when compiling alpha kernels(both of which resulted in
the hang you describe):

1. xconfig, mysteriously, does not always produce the config file I expect it
to - use menuconfig to debug, it has been more reliable for me.  I am looking
into my experience with this to determine if I am nutz,just plain stupid, or
correct.

2. make absolutely sure that the system type is selected in the config.  I
missed this a few times particularly when going back an forth between xconfig
and menuconfig.

As for media selection, the easiest way to debug this one is to compile the
lates tulip as a module (along with D. Becker's diagnostics tools on the tulip
web page).  There is a great news group associated with this as well:

http://maximus.bmen.tulane.edu/~siekas/mail.html

you can often find your problem and solution there.

The diag tool has the power, in some cases, to change the mdeia selection on
the fly.  It is a very good gia tool and inthe worst case tells Donald
everything he needs to know about why your setup is not working (it will tell
you too, on the easy ones).

Once you have compiled the tulip module, there are command line options to
choose the media when loading.	See the tulip web page (linked from mail page
link included above).  That web page also give you the ammo to look through
the tulip.c and change the "options" array to meet your media needs on a per
tulip card basis (this is a last result).

hope that helps,

/Mike
