torvalds@transmeta.com (Linus Torvalds) writes:

>You may have 1MB of L2 cache, but it's direct-mapped and physical, and
>Linux (for good reasons) doesn't try to colour it.  So depending on how
>your physical pages get spread out in memory you can't cache it all.

Which brings up a question.... Is there some way to do request memory
that maps to certain parts in the caches? Or at least to find out the
relevant address bits of some memory after having allocated it?

And if so, is there any way (hopefully short of mlock()ing everything)
to make it stay there? Maybe even portably for the 21064/066/164?

I am asking because I am playing around with UAE, the Amiga emulator, and
it is somehow disconcerting to get massively different benchmark results
when starting the exact same piece of software twice. There are a few
data structures which are used extensively, and I suspect on the slow
runs, they are kicking each other out of the caches....

Bernie
