2.0.35 164LX 533MHZ).

On the downside, as you have noticed, redhat does not include insmod or
modules on thier root floppy images.  (why, I have no idea)

I got around this by using another operating system (to remain unnamed) to
copy the files to the fat partition on this system.  I then chose local hard
drive as the installation path.

You could also use another running linux system to one of the following:

1. Copy the module files you need to get running to the root floopy you made
(could even use an x86 linux system here, these are simple file copies from
the CDROM (you are looking forthe *.o module files which should be in the
/lib/modules/preffered/  dir on the CD - I never buy them so I cannot be more
exact, but they should be on the CD somewhere).

Then copy the insmod executable to the floppy (place it in /sbin/ on the
floppy of course).  Just mount the floppy as you normally would on a linux
system.

2. Use any other operating system which might be running on this machine, or
move the SCSI drive over to anther machine and copy the files to some local
disk space and install that way.

Hope that helps.

/Mike

