hmmm...

I think you will find at some point NT does care about swapping things around
(at least it did on my system).  It did not seem to matter after a fresh
install (ie: no changes from the default setup to be lost when the NT root
drive moves ).	This behavior is the same for IDE drives on an x86 system.

It (NT) will happily boot with the root partition anywahere so long as the
bootloader code is on the first partition (c:\ in dos world), but you will
find that the registry settings are lost and it boots the "default" settings
when this path(to the root dir assuming it is not on c:\) changes.

Now back to the linux/milo problem:
Milo uses the same driver set that linux uses.  That is it compiles in the
drivers from /usr/src/linux/drivers/scsi when it is compiled.

The behavior your are describing (the order in which controller cards are
detected/configured/named is controlled by this code.  So you could recompile
milo from a running system to always call various types of controllers
/dev/sdx, but i would seem to me there is an easier solution:

Place the controller cards/drives such that it detects the added drives after
than the permanent ones.  You should be able to configure the controllers and
SCSI IDS such that (for example):

/dev/sda1 - NT/ARC boot (must be FAT for ARC)
/dev/sdax - added drives to this controller

/dev/sdb1 - Linux partition 1 (SCSI ID 0)
/dev/sdaX - additional partitions on second controller (SCSI ID X > 0)

Perhaps I am missing the point here, why is it that you cannot place the linux
partition as the first SCSI ID on the sdb controller and leave it there?

/Mike


