>John Walstra <walstra@cig.mot.com> writes:
>
>> I have some programs I want to port to the Alpha, but they contain
>> inline i386 assembly.  I figured that I could just translate the i386
>> assemble into 21164 assembly.  Of course, current I don't know 21164
>> assembly so what are some good books?  Just looking for your basic user
>> manual. I'm making a wild guess and assuming Barnes and Nobles don't
>> carry these types of books.
>... 
>
A more up-to-date reference is the "Alpha Architecture Reference Manual,
Third Edition" by Sites et al.  It *is* available from Barnes and Noble.
Go to either www.barnesandnoble.com or www.amazon.com and search for
"Alpha Architecture Reference Manual"  (make sure you find the third
edition) and you can order it for about $55.

Of course the version on the Compaq/Digital web page is cheaper.  The
web page Handbook is suitable from most user mode programming tasks but the
published Reference Manual does describe a few of the newer features
of the Alpha architecture.  The Reference Manual also includes additional
information that is necessary for writing code that executes in kernel
mode.  The Handbook only contains an overview of kernel mode programming.

 



