Success! Thanks to all who wrote to help -- Nick, Gilles, Dave, and Andreas.
Here is what worked (and what didn't):

I ftp'd the redhat 4.2 files, bootm and mboot (couldn't find them under 5.x,
and the milo I downloaded from the 5.2 directory tree would not boot from
SRM -- Dave later suggested that milo.dd, and not the raw milo image would
work).  I should mention that I didn't find it easy to get the information
I needed on the RedHat or Azstarnet sites.  A link that people may find
helpfull is:  http://www.redhat.com/linux-info/alpha/faq/FAQ-12.html
In any case, I took the two files, and gave the following commands:

 fddisk -fmt /dev/rfd0a
 cat mboot bootm > /dev/rfd0a
 disklabel -rw rfd0a 'rx23' mboot bootm

This produced a usable MILO boot floppy, bootable from the SRM console with
the command:

>>>boot dva0

A recap of things that didn't work:  I couldn't boot miata-s.gz from SRM to
the point that the ramdisk would load (hung, waiting for the keyboard).
I couldn't start ARC from SRM (in order to use this to load linux -- don't
have NT, but I thought I would try).  Once at the MILO prompt, booting the
kernel from the miata-s.img does not work, at this point I used the RedHat
5.1 miata.img file (also need the ramdisk.img file).

The miata.img and ramdisk.img files were put on floppys with the commands:

dd if=miata.img of=/dev/fd0a bs=1440k
dd if=ramdisk.img of=/dev/fd0a bs=1440k

Then I booted from the MILO prompt with the command:

MILO boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

Installation of RedHat 5.1 from sunsite.unc.edu was pretty straight forward.
At this point booting the machine is still a manual process -- boot MILO, then
boot linux with the command:

MILO boot sda5:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda5

At least i think I booted from sda5:vmlinux.gz, I may have booted from the
floppy again, but in any case, the machine is running linux!  there is still
more to do -- I need to set up X windows (need to figure out what kind of
graphics card I have, etc.), and fix it so that linux will automatically
boot when the machine is turned on, but at least at this point it is
running!  Thanks again to all who helped

