On 3 Aug 1998, J. Daniel Powell wrote:

> fine.  It is when I try ipfwadm -F -a m -S 192.168.1.0/24 -D 0.0.0.0/0
> that I get ipfwadm: setsockopt failed: Invalid argument.  Any help is
> appreciated.

There is a bug in glibc in that <netinet/ip_fw.h> has the packet and byte
counters hard-coded at 32-bit ints (u_int32_t), whereas the kernel defines
and expects "long" for those values.  This makes the glibc-structure
too small and setsockopt() returns EINVAL.  You can use the ipfwadm from
RedHat-5.0 or the one from ftp.terran.org.

cheers,
-bp
