In article <01bdbb0a$9d46eca0$7ab0448b@fmc1045>,
Jim Fredrickson <jfredrickson@hotmail.com> wrote:
>Are you using rawrite to create the milo disk?  The RH docs suggest you
>should do this, but this is incorrect.
>Format the disk as a dos/PC disk and copy the milo file and linload.exe
>using dos commands.
>Hope that helps.
>
>Nick T. <tkolya@erols.com> wrote in article
><6p0k1o$fua$1@winter.news.erols.com>...
>> I have RedHat 5.0 media, but have been unable to get any linload/milo
>> images to boot the system (from ARC). Does anyone know a quick
>> way to help get me started?


You can also boot directly off the CD-ROM.  On the RH5.0 CD, linload
is in /milo/arc/linload.exe, and the various MILO images are in /milo
(e.g. /milo/sx164). Then you tell MILO to boot from the correct image
in /kernels (e.g. /kernels/sx164.gz), and point it to the correct root
device (for the installer, just use the CD). I keep an ARC boot entry
that has everything specified.

Once you have a system that comes up, create an MS-DOS file system on your
hard disk, mount it, and copy linload.exe and the correct version of milo
to it. Then create an  ARC boot entry to boot from that partition. Be
sure to check to see if there is a newer version of MILO available from
the DEC site, and replace it on the MS-DOS file system if so.

You can switch to the "live" filesystem by using Alt-F2 to get to a shell,
then do:

$ cd /live
$ usr/sbin/chroot . /bin/sh

You might want to (re)mount the proc file system.

At that point, you should have access to all of the file utilities, a
fully populated /dev, etc. You can use fdisk to partition your drive,
mkfs.msdos to initialize the file system, and mount to mount it (you
can mount it on /mnt/floppy). You could also initialize a RAM disk and
mount that on /tmp if you need a writeable filesystem.

Fun 'n games.



