> 256k:     56204479        of 2nd level cache
> 512k:     40814057
>1024k:     32698979
>2048k:     26571787        continuously getting slower!?
>4096k:     24232887

You may have 1MB of L2 cache, but it's direct-mapped and physical, and
Linux (for good reasons) doesn't try to colour it.  So depending on how
your physical pages get spread out in memory you can't cache it all.

Anyway, it's not continuously getting slower, but the effect of the
cache is gradually getting less (you'd probably see it stagnating at
somewhere around 24000000 which would be your main memory index). 

The colouring doesn't show up for the L1, because while the L1 is
direct-mapped too, so is your memory (8kB pages), so there are no
virtual->physical translation issues for the low 13 bits of the address,
and you don't see the same effect. 

Now you know why newer CPU's tend to have multi-way associative caches. 
Much better degradation. 

			Linus



